WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.950
DYANN WIRTH: Hello, I'm Dyann Wirth.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:12.240
I'm Professor of Immunology and Infectious Disease at The Harvard TH

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.310
Chan School of Public Health.

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:19.050
And today I'm talking with Dan Hartman of the Bill and Melinda Gates

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:20.190
Foundation.

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:21.437
Dan, welcome.

00:00:21.437 --> 00:00:22.770
DAN HARTMAN: Well thanks, Dyann.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:24.240
It's a pleasure to be here.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.620
DYANN WIRTH: As director of Integrated Development,

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:31.500
what does that mean in the context of malaria eradication versus product

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.880
development?

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:37.440
DAN HARTMAN: So in product development, we are constantly

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.070
thinking about how to pull all of the different pieces

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:46.110
together, whether it's a clinical plan, a regulatory strategy,

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:50.370
a manufacturing plan, a toxicology plan.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.460
Thinking about the sequence of these various activities

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:56.730
and how to do that in the most efficient, cost effective way.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:00.490
As I look at all of the research being done on malaria,

00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:02.015
they tend to be one off.

00:01:02.015 --> 00:01:04.140
We're going to answer this question, and then we're

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:07.770
going to answer the other question, and then the subsequent question.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.590
And you have a whole bunch of questions being answered,

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:14.940
but how do you integrate all of those different results

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:19.050
into a cost effective way to eradicate malaria?

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:24.390
How do you think about the combination of various vector control tools?

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:35.670
Do I need bed nets, and IRS, and newer vector control tools?

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:38.680
Or can I substitute?

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:40.370
And how do you think about that?

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:42.990
Well if I have an effective vector control,

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:45.980
do I need to do mass drug administration?

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.690
And in what type of population?

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:50.700
And in what type of setting?

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:51.810
High intensity?

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.220
Low intensity?

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:54.090
Indoor?

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:54.930
Outdoor?

00:01:54.930 --> 00:02:00.090
There are so many different covariates, and so many pieces of information,

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:04.680
that I think this is a great opportunity to do model based global health

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.950
and try to say, what type of an environment in my entering?

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:10.440
What type of information do I have?

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:17.580
And what is the most cost effective way to utilize the tools as opposed to,

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.016
just being add on therapy.

00:02:20.016 --> 00:02:22.140
DYANN WIRTH: So when Bill and Melinda Gates in 2007

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:26.410
announced their goal to eradicate malaria, I was in the room.

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:29.850
And I remember thinking, no, it's too hard.

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:36.870
The biology, the transmission is one case leads to 100.

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:39.690
And then as I thought about it, I realized

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:42.300
it was a paradigm shift, that now you had

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:46.950
to think not about a disease but actually eradicating

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:49.660
a biological organism.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:51.580
And that's a very different thing.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:55.530
And I think that paradigm shift you see reflected now

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:58.110
in the research and the implementation.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:00.870
We are now focusing much less on disease.

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:04.860
Clearly disease is important, but we're focusing much less on disease

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:09.630
than we are on how do we get rid of the organism and its transmission.

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:11.700
DAN HARTMAN: As I've been working with the team,

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:18.390
and we fund a fair amount of research, the one question that we always ask

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:22.530
is, how will this help us eradicate malaria?

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:26.760
And that's been interesting to think through that, and the grants

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.920
that we make, and how we're approaching all of our work as opposed

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:36.940
to thinking like a physician, like I am, to OK, I see a patient,

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:38.030
I treat a patient.

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:43.050
DYANN WIRTH: You've talked a bit about evaluating the current tools in terms

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:46.080
of, will they contribute to eradication.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.340
But where do you see the holes in the toolbox?

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:52.100
Where are the missing tools?

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:57.450
DAN HARTMAN: As I've been looking at and trying to evaluate the current toolbox,

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:02.430
one of the things that I would love to see us develop,

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:06.300
is something that is less reliant on human behavior

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:11.520
because I think human behavior is so difficult to change.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:16.170
And if we look at polio, there is a human behavior

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:18.990
required for the eradication of polio.

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:22.620
You have human behavior on the part of the vaccinators.

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:27.240
They have to go out, they have to find everybody on the planet, the places

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:31.200
that they're challenged most.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:35.880
Their end game is areas with social strife.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:39.450
And in malaria because of the vector, there

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:43.260
may be opportunities to think about a tool that

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:46.470
doesn't require one human being interacting

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:50.730
with another or one human being taking the medication for a given

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:51.920
period of time.

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:57.150
And we know adherence, even in the best situations, is challenging.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:02.260
What if we could alter the vector with minimal human behavior?

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:04.340
DYANN WIRTH: It's an interesting idea.

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:08.410
It gives the opportunity to think in a completely different way,

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:13.482
not focus at all on the parasite but focus on the vector.

00:05:13.482 --> 00:05:15.940
DAN HARTMAN: I think other things that we're looking to do,

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:23.410
is to reduce the reliance on continuous medication for P Vivax.

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:28.630
Can we go from a 14 day regimen to a single dose?

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:33.700
The single dose would be, I think, beneficial in multiple different ways,

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:37.900
both for treatment because the most common regimen is

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:42.470
a BIB, three days in a row.

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:45.340
What if you could do that in a single day?

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:47.440
What would happen to resistance?

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:52.030
Because you would be getting a full dose in a single day,

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:56.125
as well as thinking about mass drug administration.

00:05:56.125 --> 00:06:00.910
If that's a tool that would help us eradicate malaria,

00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:04.300
it's a lot easier to do mass drug administration

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:09.050
if you're giving a single dose than if you have to come back multiple times.

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:13.930
So again, I think that also relates to taking the human behavior factor out

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:14.540
of it.

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:15.290
DYANN WIRTH: Good.

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:19.580
In fact, one of the challenges, as we talk about products,

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:24.130
whether it's genetically engineering mosquitoes, or it's new drugs,

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:28.570
or vaccines, one of the big issues is the regulatory issues.

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:34.000
How do you take things from discovery and get them approved?

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.020
Particularly, in situations where it's a new idea.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:44.270
It's not something that the industry has done before or rarely does.

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:46.390
And I know that you think about this a lot,

00:06:46.390 --> 00:06:50.860
and I'm wondering what your perspective is, what the approach will be?

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:53.380
DAN HARTMAN: One of the things that we try to do

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:59.770
and are doing more frequently today is, integrated development of which,

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:04.030
integrated development includes, what is your regulatory strategy.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:08.170
And it's really hard to plan if you don't know

00:07:08.170 --> 00:07:10.520
what your end is going to look like.

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:15.310
So early interactions with regulators, talking to them

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:19.510
about new products, what could these products be,

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:24.760
what could the endpoints be, and then developing a strategy on how

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:27.970
to get there in the most effective way.

00:07:27.970 --> 00:07:31.910
Again remembering, that most products fail.

00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:35.410
So if you don't know where you're going, and you

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:38.230
don't know what the steps are going to get there, if you do

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:42.200
fail you're likely to fail late as opposed to failing early,

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:44.440
which is a very expensive endeavor.

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:47.710
Being able to make a clear case as to why

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:51.670
you think a product would be safe and effective

00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:55.650
or have an appropriate risk benefit in a given situation,

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:59.110
is obviously necessary.

00:07:59.110 --> 00:08:04.960
And then having the open discussions with various regulatory agencies,

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:12.560
and sometimes I see us thinking more developed world regulatory agencies.

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:15.160
But what we really need to do is to go to the communities

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:19.690
that we work and get their perspectives on a risk benefit.

00:08:19.690 --> 00:08:22.390
DYANN WIRTH: I think that's a very important point

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:25.510
because it is a different population that's

00:08:25.510 --> 00:08:28.690
going to uptake almost everything that's developed

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:32.110
during this phase of the elimination and eradication campaign.

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:35.320
DAN HARTMAN: I had a fascinating discussion with one of my colleagues

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:42.080
from Senegal, and he was telling me about malaria in the 1990s.

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:47.530
And he was one of the chief physicians at this hospital in Senegal.

00:08:47.530 --> 00:08:51.160
And chloroquine resistance hit their area, and they said,

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:55.960
I would sign 20 to 50 death certificates a day.

00:08:55.960 --> 00:09:01.030
I don't have that experience, and a lot of regulators in the developed world

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:02.210
don't have that experience.

00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:05.350
So when you start thinking about risk and benefit,

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:09.250
unless you have that perspective and you've lived in those communities,

00:09:09.250 --> 00:09:10.640
it's really hard to have that.

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:13.600
So that's one of the things that we're starting to do.

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:16.660
Especially with novel products, is really get out into the communities

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:21.310
where they're going to be used and get their perspectives early on

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:27.280
and try to have, whether it's WHO, stringent regulatory agencies

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:31.810
from developed worlds, national regulatory agencies from countries

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:37.480
where it will be utilized eventually, all have that discussion together.

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:41.000
We've made some really good progress in that area.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:46.780
We've been working on a project called African Regulatory Medicine

00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:54.190
Harmonization, which is taking a regional approach to a country

00:09:54.190 --> 00:09:57.520
approach for product introduction.

00:09:57.520 --> 00:09:59.350
DYANN WIRTH: Because most of the countries

00:09:59.350 --> 00:10:03.550
don't have a very well-developed regulatory agency.

00:10:03.550 --> 00:10:05.970
There are a few, but for the most part in Africa,

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:08.700
in particular, other than South Africa, I

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:12.930
think there's relatively little local expertise in country.

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:16.809
But from a regional perspective, that could be of interest.

00:10:16.809 --> 00:10:19.600
DAN HARTMAN: We're trying to do multiple things with this approach.

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:23.520
So one is to get a critical mass, as you were describing.

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:26.610
So if we can take the East African community,

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:29.070
pull their resources and their best people

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:35.700
together, and have them do Dossier reviews, that's a positive thing.

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:37.800
The way it works, we've had it set up right now

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:41.550
as we're bringing in other regulators, whether it's

00:10:41.550 --> 00:10:46.350
Swissmedic, or folks from WHO, or other stringent regulatory agencies

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:49.860
to help answer questions that they might have.

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:56.790
And they're also relying on other stringent regulatory agency reviews.

00:10:56.790 --> 00:10:59.250
So instead of starting from scratch, they're

00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:03.400
looking at somebody else's report, ideally, and saying, OK,

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:05.790
do I agree with this report as opposed to having

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:08.880
to dig through an entire dossier?

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:12.840
And the whole purpose of that is to make sure you have a quality review

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:14.940
and make sure it's timely.

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:17.680
But it also benefits manufacturers.

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:21.030
I was a manufacturer in my old role, and I

00:11:21.030 --> 00:11:26.140
wanted to submit a drug to Sub-Saharan Africa, I would have over 50 dossiers,

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:30.390
that I would have to prepare and submit, and get questions back

00:11:30.390 --> 00:11:36.810
from 50 countries, respond to all 50 for a market with very

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:39.300
marginal turn on that.

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:44.370
Well we can create a system where we have four or five regional areas

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:51.000
and you can drop in four or five dossiers, as opposed to 50,

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:56.700
get back four or five sets of questions and have a general format

00:11:56.700 --> 00:11:58.620
that everybody's agreed to.

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:04.540
That would dramatically reduce the burden on the manufacturers.

00:12:04.540 --> 00:12:08.340
We did a study about three years ago, looking

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:14.880
at the time it takes for a product to go through the entire regulatory system

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:17.910
and get to people in developing countries.

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:22.920
In developed countries, it's about nine to 12 months.

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:28.170
In developing countries, especially in Sub-Saharan Africa,

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:31.410
we found it's about four to seven years.

00:12:31.410 --> 00:12:33.390
And it's usually approved in the developed

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:37.410
world, goes to WHO for pre-qualification,

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:43.680
then it goes to the national regulatory agencies for their review and approval.

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:46.740
Frequently, that none of those three groups

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:49.465
were taking advantage of prior reviews.

00:12:49.465 --> 00:12:51.840
DYANN WIRTH: So they were doing de novo review each time?

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:54.180
DAN HARTMAN: De novo review each time.

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:58.680
And that obviously, lengthened it by 3x, just

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:02.260
if you did that in the ideal situation.

00:13:02.260 --> 00:13:04.860
The other thing that we found is manufacturers

00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:08.060
because they had to apply to each country.

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:11.370
The spread of their submissions of dossiers

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:12.739
was sometimes up to four years.

00:13:12.739 --> 00:13:13.530
DYANN WIRTH: Right.

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:15.745
Because it had to fit into their workflow.

00:13:15.745 --> 00:13:16.620
DAN HARTMAN: Exactly.

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:19.290
What we're trying to do through this harmonization effort,

00:13:19.290 --> 00:13:26.130
is to support mutual reliance and minimize, I would say,

00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:30.870
non-value added activity of applying to each different country as opposed

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:31.740
to regions.

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:35.970
And we've had some great success in East Africa.

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:39.870
And now this approach in this harmonization effort

00:13:39.870 --> 00:13:42.030
is moving into West Africa.

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:45.450
South Africa already had started something on their own.

00:13:45.450 --> 00:13:50.790
At WHO pre-qualification, we found that the drug side was relying on stringent

00:13:50.790 --> 00:13:55.420
regulatory agency reviews, but the vaccine side wasn't.

00:13:55.420 --> 00:13:57.390
And there was about a 13 month difference

00:13:57.390 --> 00:14:00.060
in review time between drug and vaccines.

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:09.960
That was a relatively easy change to do and now the timelines are much better.

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:15.470
Looking at mutual reliance of national regulatory agencies

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:20.210
on either, WHO, or other stringent regulatory agencies reports,

00:14:20.210 --> 00:14:25.950
has again streamlined it, single dossiers as opposed to multiple.

00:14:25.950 --> 00:14:32.370
So all of these things have reduced the overall timelines by close to 50%,

00:14:32.370 --> 00:14:34.210
to what they were three years ago.

00:14:34.210 --> 00:14:38.700
DYANN WIRTH: And it also strikes me that there is an enormous capacity building

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:45.270
potential here because as this develops, perhaps, the Africans will even

00:14:45.270 --> 00:14:50.730
be able to do standalone approval at the highest level

00:14:50.730 --> 00:14:55.830
because at some point perhaps, that's the right strategy for registering

00:14:55.830 --> 00:14:59.670
a product that's going to be either, primarily

00:14:59.670 --> 00:15:03.867
or only used in a malaria endemic country.

00:15:03.867 --> 00:15:06.200
DAN HARTMAN: I think that really is where we want to go.

00:15:06.200 --> 00:15:10.700
And the regional approach makes sense to try

00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:18.350
to stand up 50 plus high quality regulatory agencies

00:15:18.350 --> 00:15:22.285
even within the next decade, I think would be almost impossible.

00:15:22.285 --> 00:15:23.160
DYANN WIRTH: Exactly.

00:15:23.160 --> 00:15:26.090
And also not productive because malaria is

00:15:26.090 --> 00:15:29.060
going to have to be solved on a regional level.

00:15:29.060 --> 00:15:30.890
The disease doesn't know borders.

00:15:30.890 --> 00:15:36.140
That's something that's becoming accepted as a mode of interaction

00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:37.370
between countries.

00:15:37.370 --> 00:15:42.080
What advice would you give to people early in their career,

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:45.140
or even pre-career, what kinds of things do

00:15:45.140 --> 00:15:48.160
you think are important for people to think about?

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:50.700
DAN HARTMAN: That's a great question.

00:15:50.700 --> 00:15:55.010
I think that as scientists and physician scientists,

00:15:55.010 --> 00:15:58.880
we underestimate the importance of leadership training.

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:05.510
I went through 15 years of post-high school education, and 99% of it

00:16:05.510 --> 00:16:06.830
was science based.

00:16:06.830 --> 00:16:10.820
The leadership component, is a totally different component,

00:16:10.820 --> 00:16:14.180
and I think it's one that can be learned.

00:16:14.180 --> 00:16:19.310
For the last almost 20 years, I read some type of leadership book

00:16:19.310 --> 00:16:20.540
every quarter.

00:16:20.540 --> 00:16:26.480
I take time out of my busy schedule to take some type of course

00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:30.920
that I think will help my career development, whether it's I've

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:35.450
taken courses on implementation management, change management,

00:16:35.450 --> 00:16:39.800
decision quality, a variety of leadership courses.

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:42.710
It has really helped my career and helped

00:16:42.710 --> 00:16:46.160
me understand what good leadership looks like

00:16:46.160 --> 00:16:50.660
and how I can constantly improve myself.

00:16:50.660 --> 00:16:54.020
I think career development has to start with the individual.

00:16:54.020 --> 00:16:56.780
Nobody is going to develop your career for you.

00:16:56.780 --> 00:17:04.160
The sooner I realize that, then I could start taking control over my own career

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:08.030
development, understand what some of my areas of weakness

00:17:08.030 --> 00:17:11.780
were, how am I going to fix those, and develop.

00:17:11.780 --> 00:17:20.990
That has made a significant difference in terms of how I think, how I work.

00:17:20.990 --> 00:17:27.109
Early on in my career, I think it was less important, but today most of what

00:17:27.108 --> 00:17:30.710
I try to do is influence my environment.

00:17:30.710 --> 00:17:37.640
And certainly, I try to do that through scientific discussions and arguments,

00:17:37.640 --> 00:17:38.690
if you will.

00:17:38.690 --> 00:17:41.330
But a lot of it is, I think, some of the softer

00:17:41.330 --> 00:17:45.620
skills, whether it's an emotional intelligence component

00:17:45.620 --> 00:17:52.100
or how do you think about decision quality, which is something

00:17:52.100 --> 00:17:53.330
that we tend to overlook.

00:17:53.330 --> 00:17:58.340
We think we all make great decisions, but you can actually tell,

00:17:58.340 --> 00:18:03.140
did you have a quality process in your decision making at the time

00:18:03.140 --> 00:18:06.380
you make the decision irrespective of outcome.

00:18:06.380 --> 00:18:08.750
DYANN WIRTH: Interesting advice because I agree,

00:18:08.750 --> 00:18:13.250
we don't spend a lot of time teaching people how to lead

00:18:13.250 --> 00:18:16.590
or systematically how to make decisions.

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:21.710
We know how to test a scientific hypothesis or follow a protocol,

00:18:21.710 --> 00:18:26.180
but we don't focus on how do you get to decisions,

00:18:26.180 --> 00:18:28.250
and how do you lead people to decisions.

00:18:28.250 --> 00:18:29.360
Well thank you very much.

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:33.780
It was wonderful that you could spend time with our students.

00:18:33.780 --> 00:18:35.590
DAN HARTMAN: Thank you Dyann.

